+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for
+days, `h` for hours, `m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may
+appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces, so `2d`, `1h30m`,
+`90m`, and `1d2h3m4s` are all valid. Units are case-insensitive.
+
+Negative values, decimals like `1.5h`, and unknown, repeated, or out of order
+units are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default is used
+unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
